I updated the issue title. As for:

{quote}
we need a way to disable the tour per request / page load (i.e. when performing the PDF export)
{quote}

I actually think it's cleaner if the PDF export doesn't know about the tour, i.e. we don't disable the tour from the PDF export. Instead, the tour should not initialize itself on the export action. ATM the tour JavaScript code is loaded on every page (it's a JSX used "On this wiki") and it fetches the steps for the current document, initializing the tour when that list is not empty. For now I would simply add a check to prevent the tour initialization if the context action is export.

Note that this redirect is problematic in other cases to. For instance you can't access (directly, using the URL) the home page history until you dismiss the tour. In general, any URL that targets the home page (besides the default {{/view/Main/}}) will be redirected. I think this is a bad design. I find it safer to trigger the tour (automatically) only when the step URL is matched, i.e. only when the default {{/view/Main/}} URL is loaded. When the URL doesn't match I'd let the user trigger the tour manually.

description:
*Steps to reproduce*

# As a user with programming rights, create a new AWM application with a field of type short text.
# As a user with just edit rights, create a new entry in the AWM application with content 
{noformat}
{{async async="true" cached="false" context="doc.reference"}}{{groovy}}println("Hello from groovy!"){{/groovy}}{{/async}}
{noformat}
in the short text field

*Expected result*

The value
{noformat}
{{async async="true" cached="false" context="doc.reference"}}{{groovy}}println("Hello from groovy!"){{/groovy}}{{/async}}
{noformat}
is displayed in the text field.

*Actual result*

The value {{Hello from groovy!}} is displayed.

This demonstrates a privilege escalation attack from edit to programming rights. This can be exploited on any AWM application that has been created by a user with programming rights and contains a text field that is not the title. I haven't checked if other fields are exploitable, too. From what I've seen, the demo applications don't contain such a field. As this should be a common situation, I don't see that this limits the applicability of the attack.

The affects version is only the version I reproduced this on, this should also affect much older versions of XWiki.

*Steps to reproduce*

# Log in as a user without script rights.
# Set your first name to 

{noformat}
{{cache id="userProfile"}}{{groovy}}println("Hello from groovy!"){{/groovy}}{{/cache}}
{noformat}

*Expected result:*

{noformat}
{{cache id="userProfile"}}{{groovy}}println("Hello from groovy!"){{/groovy}}{{/cache}}
{noformat}

is displayed as first name.

*Actual result:*

The username in the profile is {{Hello from groovy!}}

This demonstrates a privilege escalation attack to programming rights. I don't know yet why or how this works as this was primarily a surprise result while trying to reproduce another vulnerability.

I've also verified that you can actually do dangerous stuff, for example

{noformat}
{{async async="true" cached="false" context="doc.reference"}}{{velocity}}#set($descriptor = $services.wiki.currentWikiDescriptor)) #set($void = $descriptor.setOwnerId("XWiki.NewOwner")) #set($void = $services.wiki.saveDescriptor($descriptor)){{/velocity}}{{/async}}
{noformat}

sets the wiki's owner to the given user id.

The affects version is only the version I reproduced the issue on, this is quite likely much older.

h2. Steps to reproduce

In a subwiki:
* Create page "Sandbox.ABC.Test1.WebHome"
* Create page "Sandbox.ABC.Test2.WebHome" with the following content:
{noformat}
{{include reference="Sandbox.ABC.Test1.WebHome"/}}
{noformat}
* Move page "Sandbox.ABC.Test1.WebHome" to "Sandbox.ABC.Test3.WebHome"

h2. Expected result

The content of "Sandbox.ABC.Test2.WebHome" is updated to the following:
{noformat}
{{include reference="Sandbox.ABC.Test3.WebHome"/}}
{noformat}

h2. Actual result

The content of "Sandbox.ABC.Test2.WebHome" remains unchanged.

Note that when performing the same test on the main wiki, the reference is updated as expected.

