It seems the stacktrace from the error displayed in [^Guest_user_Error_commentsinline.vm_3rd_comment.png] is almost the same as the one from XWIKI-18717, but the NPE description seems to be different:
{code:java}
Caused by: java.lang.NullPointerException: Cannot read field "parent" because "reference" is null{code}

Just reproduced it also on a XWiki 12.10.11 instance (Firefox 96/ PostgreSQL 14/ Tomcat 9.0.56).

The failing code is basically

{code}
{{velocity}}
$datetool.timeZone.getOffset($datetool.date.time)
{{/velocity}}
{code}

Called in https://github.com/xwiki/xwiki-platform/blob/518c47cdebb178bd44614b61b8e4713cdc8023b0/xwiki-platform-core/xwiki-platform-search/xwiki-platform-search-solr/xwiki-platform-search-solr-ui/src/main/resources/Main/SolrDateFacet.xml#L180.

I don't quite understand the error yet as "sun.util.calendar.ZoneInfo" is not referenced directly, it just happen to be the implementation of "java.util.TimeZone" which is not even deprecated.

The issue reproduces also on a Standard Jetty+HSQLDB instance as well. I first reproduced it on a local Tomcat 9.0.56 created instance with XWiki 13.10 (started with default Tomcat settings): 
{code:java}
NOTE: Picked up JDK_JAVA_OPTIONS:  --add-opens=java.base/java.lang=ALL-UNNAMED --add-opens=java.base/java.io=ALL-UNNAMED --add-opens=java.base/java.util=ALL-UNNAMED --add-opens=java.base/java.util.concurrent=ALL-UNNAMED --add-opens=java.rmi/sun.rmi.transport=ALL-UNNAMED{code}

What is interesting is that the exact same code does not fail in Java or Groovy, it's only when executed through Velocity.

It kind of feel like the difference would be that Java/Groovy calls the method as {{java.util.TimeZone#getOffset}} while Velocity calls directly {{sun.util.calendar.ZoneInfo#getOffset}}.

The following (which is basically what Velocity does) also reproduce the problem:

{noformat}
TimeZone.getDefault().getClass().getMethod("getOffset", long.class).invoke(TimeZone.getDefault(), 0);
{noformat}

but the following does not:

{noformat}
TimeZone.class.getMethod("getOffset", long.class).invoke(TimeZone.getDefault(), 0);
{noformat}

The difference is well illustrated by this Groovy script:

{noformat}
{{groovy}}
println TimeZone.getDefault().getClass().getMethod("getOffset", long.class)
println TimeZone.class.getMethod("getOffset", long.class)
{{/groovy}}
{noformat}

which produces:

{noformat}
public int sun.util.calendar.ZoneInfo.getOffset(long)
public int java.util.TimeZone.getOffset(long)
{noformat}

I created https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/VELOCITY-952 to discuss a possible generic fix at Velocity level for this problem.

